#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055799} :activity/id #uuid "59d38b59-6b69-574e-b424-1725c191f15d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725 :topic/id #uuid "b8bf3ca3-98f8-5ec7-8c13-6f1988c58838" :topic/math-academy-id 65} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055799 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056026 :tutorial/content "Solving a linear equation involves **isolating** the variable on one side of the equation. \n\nThis can often be accomplished using the **addition principle**. The addition principle tells us that adding (or subtracting) the same number on both sides of an equation does not change its solution.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 5.\n$$\n\nThe variable $x$ is not isolated, because we also have a $+4$ on the left-hand side. \n\nTo isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *subtracting* $4$ from $x.$ This way, the $+4$ and $-4$ will cancel out to $0,$ which will leave $x$ alone. Remember to do this to *both* sides of the equation! \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx + 4 &= 5 \\\n    \t\tx+4\\,{\\color{red}{-}}\\,{\\color{red}{4}} &=5\\,{\\color{red}{-}}\\,{\\color{red}{4}} \\\n\t\tx+(4-4) &=(5-4) \\\n\t\tx + 0 &= 1 \\\n\t\tx &= 1.\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is our solution. Let's check that our solution is correct by plugging it back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\tx + 4 &= 5 \\\n\t\t1 + 4&= 5 \\\n\t\t5 &=5 \\;{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1eab91ef-23e4-5e41-9ef4-72aa3c0eb594" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8126 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "eb443e30-c901-5b2a-8da7-3b718c082035" :step/math-academy-id 52727 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054781 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054411 :question/id #uuid "ca811d3a-a7be-536b-b997-dd2355cb00d4" :question/math-academy-id 